WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.280 --> 00:00:02.490
- Good afternoon everyone.

00:00:02.490 --> 00:00:03.970
I am Saad Omer

00:00:03.970 --> 00:00:06.830
and the Director of the Yale
Institute for Global Health.

00:00:06.830 --> 00:00:09.790
And I'm delighted to welcome you

00:00:09.790 --> 00:00:12.630
to the YGH Global Health
conversation series.

00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:15.323
Our latest event in this series.

00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:19.570
We, in addition to YGH we are indebted

00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:22.580
to the George Herbert
Walker Jr. Lecture series

00:00:23.950 --> 00:00:28.583
that is supported by the Macmillan center.

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:32.600
Today, I'm pleased to be
joined by my co-moderator

00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:34.233
Dr. Linda Arnold.

00:00:35.230 --> 00:00:37.940
She's an Associate Professor of Pediatrics

00:00:38.830 --> 00:00:42.253
and Emergency Medicine at
Yale School of Medicine.

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:45.090
She's a former Peace Corps volunteer

00:00:45.090 --> 00:00:47.500
and she has been actively engaged

00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:49.520
in global health and education.

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:51.470
In fact, I, you know,
the first time I met her,

00:00:51.470 --> 00:00:56.130
we were both in Panama and her effort,

00:00:56.130 --> 00:00:58.593
and she was doing work
on capacity building.

00:00:59.683 --> 00:01:00.516
And in addition to that,

00:01:00.516 --> 00:01:02.310
she has worked on program development

00:01:02.310 --> 00:01:05.380
for more than 30 years.

00:01:05.380 --> 00:01:09.280
She's also the North
American representative

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:10.250
to the standing committee

00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:13.030
of the International
Pediatric Association.

00:01:13.030 --> 00:01:15.610
Welcome Linda, it's great
to have you with us today.

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:17.370
And as a reminder

00:01:17.370 --> 00:01:22.370
all attendees have their
volumes and video features muted

00:01:24.010 --> 00:01:27.253
but of course, we are eager
to incorporate your questions.

00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:30.200
You can simply submit those questions

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:33.220
using the question and answer feature.

00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:35.330
We are also recording today's session

00:01:35.330 --> 00:01:36.800
and it will be available

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:40.823
on YouTube and the YGH Facebook page.

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:44.610
And now let me introduced everyone,

00:01:44.610 --> 00:01:48.740
to today's distinguished
guest Henrietta Fore.

00:01:48.740 --> 00:01:53.650
Who's the seventh executive
director of UNICEF,

00:01:53.650 --> 00:01:57.293
which, a position she
began in January, 2018.

00:01:58.170 --> 00:02:01.050
She is not new to global
health and development.

00:02:01.050 --> 00:02:04.390
And she's been a real champion
of economic development

00:02:04.390 --> 00:02:08.180
education, health, and
humanitarian assistance

00:02:08.180 --> 00:02:12.100
as well as disaster relief
in a public service,

00:02:12.100 --> 00:02:13.810
as well as a private sector

00:02:13.810 --> 00:02:17.590
a non profit leadership career

00:02:17.590 --> 00:02:19.963
that spans more than four decades.

00:02:20.930 --> 00:02:23.080
One of her career highlights

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:26.640
is that she was the,
between 2007 and 2009,

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:29.579
she was the executive director,

00:02:29.579 --> 00:02:33.963
well she was the
administrator of the USAID.

00:02:35.490 --> 00:02:39.069
And she was the first woman to serve

00:02:39.069 --> 00:02:42.880
in this role and was responsible for

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:46.400
managing approximately $40 billion

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:50.052
of US foreign assistance annually.

00:02:50.052 --> 00:02:52.880
Including support to peoples and countries

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:54.820
recovering from disasters,

00:02:54.820 --> 00:02:56.250
building their futures

00:02:56.250 --> 00:02:59.420
economically, politically and socially

00:03:00.260 --> 00:03:02.556
Before her career at USAID,

00:03:02.556 --> 00:03:06.310
she was appointed assistant
administrator for Asia

00:03:06.310 --> 00:03:08.490
and assistant administrator
for private enterprise

00:03:08.490 --> 00:03:09.680
in the same agency.

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:12.560
She serves on the boards of

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:14.740
Overseas Private Investment Corporation

00:03:14.740 --> 00:03:17.050
and the Millennium Challenge Corporation.

00:03:17.050 --> 00:03:21.900
And in, 2009 Executive Director Fore

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:24.160
received the distinguished service award

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:28.320
the highest award the
secretary of state can bestow.

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:31.200
So without further ado

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:33.560
you know, I wanna welcome
Executive Director Fore

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:36.910
and, you know, look
forward to talking about

00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:42.293
some of the issues that
we'll be focusing on.

00:03:45.204 --> 00:03:48.983
In terms of the way it will go.

00:03:50.460 --> 00:03:52.610
We would appreciate,

00:03:52.610 --> 00:03:57.580
we'll start with Director
Fore's remarks, brief remarks

00:03:57.580 --> 00:04:00.690
and then we'll have a
question answer session,

00:04:00.690 --> 00:04:04.760
which Dr. Arnold and I will moderate.

00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:07.543
And we'll also take audience questions.

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:11.970
- Well, thank you very much.

00:04:11.970 --> 00:04:15.900
Saad, I really appreciate
it and I will start formally

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:19.290
and then let's get into
a good conversation.

00:04:19.290 --> 00:04:21.780
So thank you very much.

00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:23.450
And also thank you very much

00:04:23.450 --> 00:04:26.060
to the Yale Institute for Global Health

00:04:26.060 --> 00:04:27.880
for welcoming me here today.

00:04:27.880 --> 00:04:30.670
We have a number of programs
that we do together,

00:04:30.670 --> 00:04:32.130
which are all exciting

00:04:32.130 --> 00:04:36.340
and they lead to, in
many paths of the future.

00:04:36.340 --> 00:04:38.050
So they lead to many areas

00:04:38.050 --> 00:04:39.387
that we're going to be talking about.

00:04:39.387 --> 00:04:41.100
And so I appreciate it.

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:43.663
We appreciate it at UNICEF very much.

00:04:44.830 --> 00:04:48.590
I am looking forward to
discussing with all of you,

00:04:48.590 --> 00:04:52.040
what is truly an emergency of our time,

00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:54.520
and it is, COVID-19.

00:04:54.520 --> 00:04:57.000
What we need to do as a global community

00:04:57.000 --> 00:04:59.350
to support children's health and wellbeing

00:04:59.350 --> 00:05:04.260
through and importantly,
beyond this extraordinary time.

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:08.630
And I say a global
community very deliberately,

00:05:08.630 --> 00:05:11.230
building and strengthening
systems for the future

00:05:11.230 --> 00:05:12.640
cannot be done alone

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:15.960
by governments or agencies like UNICEF.

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:19.200
It requires the ideas and the innovations

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:23.520
the funding, the collaboration
of an ecosystem of partners,

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:25.770
all working together.

00:05:25.770 --> 00:05:28.490
Collaboration has
certainly been a hallmark

00:05:28.490 --> 00:05:32.700
of the Yale UNICEF partnership
across collaborative ties

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:37.220
between us are strong and
also they are growing.

00:05:37.220 --> 00:05:39.980
UNICEF has benefited
from Yale's leadership

00:05:39.980 --> 00:05:43.320
in global health fellowship
with two Yale students

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:46.750
who've been working in our
health section this past summer

00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:50.270
Nicholas Alipui, Yale's lecturer

00:05:50.270 --> 00:05:52.780
at the Jackson Institute
for Global Affairs

00:05:52.780 --> 00:05:55.800
was a long time senior leader at UNICEF.

00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:58.040
And he is now bringing his expertise

00:05:58.040 --> 00:06:03.040
to the Capstone course on
non-communicable diseases at Yale.

00:06:03.800 --> 00:06:06.910
And every year, UNICEF
welcomes Yale's experts

00:06:06.910 --> 00:06:08.980
in a diverse number of areas.

00:06:08.980 --> 00:06:10.880
From child nutrition and feeding

00:06:10.880 --> 00:06:14.520
to early childhood development to HIV AIDS

00:06:14.520 --> 00:06:17.310
and of critical importance today

00:06:17.310 --> 00:06:19.660
the vaccine demand laboratory,

00:06:19.660 --> 00:06:20.790
which Saad is leading

00:06:20.790 --> 00:06:24.013
with one of UNICEF,
senior social scientists.

00:06:24.890 --> 00:06:28.210
But I hope that we can continue
to do even more together

00:06:28.210 --> 00:06:30.080
in the years ahead.

00:06:30.080 --> 00:06:33.940
In fact, the diversity of
thinking around the many areas

00:06:33.940 --> 00:06:36.070
of children's health and wellbeing

00:06:36.070 --> 00:06:39.973
is part of what I would like
to leave with you today.

00:06:40.890 --> 00:06:43.380
We cannot support children's health

00:06:43.380 --> 00:06:47.330
if we fail to support all
of the other ingredients

00:06:47.330 --> 00:06:50.650
that support their
development and wellbeing.

00:06:50.650 --> 00:06:54.560
Education, protection
from violence and abuse,

00:06:54.560 --> 00:06:57.810
water and sanitation, mental health,

00:06:57.810 --> 00:07:01.950
nutrition, social protections,
skills development,

00:07:01.950 --> 00:07:04.830
so they can prepare for the world of work.

00:07:04.830 --> 00:07:07.220
All of these ingredients help support

00:07:07.220 --> 00:07:10.190
optimal health and development.

00:07:10.190 --> 00:07:12.940
And all of the systems that
support these ingredients

00:07:12.940 --> 00:07:16.970
came under attack with COVID-19.

00:07:16.970 --> 00:07:19.570
The unprecedented measures
that we have taken

00:07:19.570 --> 00:07:21.870
to contain the virus.

00:07:21.870 --> 00:07:25.830
Schools were closed leaving
1.6 billion children

00:07:25.830 --> 00:07:27.360
out of school,

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:32.360
immunization campaigns around
the world ground to a halt,

00:07:32.420 --> 00:07:35.740
parents stopped taking their
children to medical centers

00:07:35.740 --> 00:07:36.880
because they were afraid

00:07:36.880 --> 00:07:39.950
of coming into contact with the virus.

00:07:39.950 --> 00:07:43.570
Households were hit hard
by the economic impact.

00:07:43.570 --> 00:07:46.600
Families are finding it
difficult to make ends meet

00:07:46.600 --> 00:07:51.600
for even the most important
and basic necessities like food

00:07:51.940 --> 00:07:55.230
and children and women have
become more exposed to violence

00:07:55.230 --> 00:07:59.773
and abuse online, but also
within their own households.

00:08:00.670 --> 00:08:02.400
But what about those places

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:06.940
where these systems were already
weak or even non-existent?

00:08:06.940 --> 00:08:10.370
Places where primary health
care is a day's journey

00:08:10.370 --> 00:08:13.240
or more from where people live,

00:08:13.240 --> 00:08:17.920
or crowded conditions make it
impossible to self distance

00:08:17.920 --> 00:08:22.600
where a family of seven or
even 10 share a single room,

00:08:22.600 --> 00:08:24.790
where humanitarian emergencies

00:08:24.790 --> 00:08:27.240
like conflicts make reaching children

00:08:27.240 --> 00:08:30.890
with basic services like
vaccinations or treatment

00:08:30.890 --> 00:08:35.333
for severe acute malnutrition
extraordinarily difficult.

00:08:36.760 --> 00:08:40.570
Where one of the best
defenses against COVID-19,

00:08:40.570 --> 00:08:43.530
hand-washing, is simply out of reach

00:08:43.530 --> 00:08:46.793
because of lack of clean
water and a bar of soap.

00:08:47.650 --> 00:08:49.670
COVID-19 has reminded us all

00:08:49.670 --> 00:08:53.030
of the stark inequalities around the world

00:08:53.030 --> 00:08:55.850
between the haves and the have-nots

00:08:55.850 --> 00:08:57.700
and in the most extreme cases

00:08:57.700 --> 00:09:01.890
places of conflict or
drought, for example, in Yemen

00:09:01.890 --> 00:09:03.400
or in the Sahil,

00:09:03.400 --> 00:09:07.160
or for refugees living
in overcrowded camps.

00:09:07.160 --> 00:09:11.660
And the pandemic is making
that situation even worse.

00:09:11.660 --> 00:09:16.240
In many ways, UNICEF was made
for this kind of an emergency

00:09:16.240 --> 00:09:19.490
with offices in over 190 countries

00:09:19.490 --> 00:09:21.980
our staff members are
working shoulder to shoulder

00:09:21.980 --> 00:09:24.800
with governments to deliver nutrition,

00:09:24.800 --> 00:09:27.710
to vaccinate children, provide water,

00:09:27.710 --> 00:09:31.410
emergency healthcare, education
and protection services

00:09:31.410 --> 00:09:33.280
to the displaced.

00:09:33.280 --> 00:09:36.450
Our staff members and
partners, are also working

00:09:36.450 --> 00:09:38.690
to give development a foothold

00:09:38.690 --> 00:09:42.770
in parts of the world where
a vaccination, a bed net

00:09:42.770 --> 00:09:46.350
a drink of clean water,
a cup of nutritious food

00:09:46.350 --> 00:09:49.620
a seat at a classroom or a paved road

00:09:49.620 --> 00:09:52.520
can seem like a distant dream.

00:09:52.520 --> 00:09:55.890
And we're directly supporting
the COVID-19 response

00:09:55.890 --> 00:09:57.690
on a number of fronts.

00:09:57.690 --> 00:10:00.070
We're delivering life saving supplies.

00:10:00.070 --> 00:10:01.570
We're working with communities

00:10:01.570 --> 00:10:05.890
to provide information and
messaging to prevent infection.

00:10:05.890 --> 00:10:08.910
We're providing online
and distance learning

00:10:08.910 --> 00:10:11.970
to millions of out of school children.

00:10:11.970 --> 00:10:15.580
We're keeping vaccination
campaigns up and running.

00:10:15.580 --> 00:10:19.100
We're providing water,
sanitation and hygiene supplies

00:10:19.100 --> 00:10:22.460
to communities that are battling COVID-19.

00:10:22.460 --> 00:10:23.790
We're playing the lead role

00:10:23.790 --> 00:10:28.380
in the ACT Accelerator
initiative at the COVAX facility

00:10:28.380 --> 00:10:30.660
to ensure that any vaccine is delivered

00:10:30.660 --> 00:10:33.900
fairly and inequitably around the world.

00:10:33.900 --> 00:10:35.250
And throughout we're working

00:10:35.250 --> 00:10:36.890
shoulder to shoulder with governments

00:10:36.890 --> 00:10:40.030
as they rebuild stronger
schools, hospitals,

00:10:40.030 --> 00:10:44.410
resilient water and sanitation
services for the future.

00:10:44.410 --> 00:10:45.860
And we're helping them to strengthen

00:10:45.860 --> 00:10:48.610
lasting community-based health systems

00:10:48.610 --> 00:10:49.960
where all of the services

00:10:49.960 --> 00:10:52.250
that children and their families need

00:10:52.250 --> 00:10:56.140
from vaccinations, to
nutrition, to obstetric care

00:10:56.140 --> 00:10:58.830
can be found in one place

00:10:58.830 --> 00:11:01.610
because supporting children's
health and wellbeing

00:11:01.610 --> 00:11:05.120
depends on supporting the whole child,

00:11:05.120 --> 00:11:07.400
their health, but also their development

00:11:07.400 --> 00:11:11.340
their education, and
their access to services.

00:11:11.340 --> 00:11:14.430
Today, I look forward to
discussing how UNICEF is working

00:11:14.430 --> 00:11:16.720
with our partners around the world

00:11:16.720 --> 00:11:20.000
public and private alike
to meet these needs

00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:22.110
and to use this moment as a chance

00:11:22.110 --> 00:11:26.870
to re-imagine the systems
that children are counting on.

00:11:26.870 --> 00:11:29.160
But we'd also like to hear from you.

00:11:29.160 --> 00:11:32.120
I'm sure that many of
you will answer the call

00:11:32.120 --> 00:11:34.120
to public and global service

00:11:34.120 --> 00:11:36.830
in the health field and beyond.

00:11:36.830 --> 00:11:40.060
Perhaps you'll go to work in
agencies and organizations

00:11:40.060 --> 00:11:43.440
dedicated to the education
and economic empowerment

00:11:43.440 --> 00:11:44.990
of young people

00:11:44.990 --> 00:11:47.690
or become development professionals

00:11:47.690 --> 00:11:51.120
diplomats, policy professionals, NGO,

00:11:51.120 --> 00:11:54.160
or civil society workers, innovators,

00:11:54.160 --> 00:11:56.850
business people, and teachers.

00:11:56.850 --> 00:11:58.990
So please let us know your thoughts,

00:11:58.990 --> 00:12:02.780
contribute any research
or ideas you might have

00:12:02.780 --> 00:12:04.710
on solutions or technologies

00:12:04.710 --> 00:12:07.970
that might better serve
children and young people.

00:12:07.970 --> 00:12:09.450
Consider doing a report

00:12:09.450 --> 00:12:13.020
on the issues affecting
their lives and futures

00:12:13.020 --> 00:12:16.520
and send us your ideas on innovations.

00:12:16.520 --> 00:12:18.600
We need you on this journey with us,

00:12:18.600 --> 00:12:21.900
through your work you can make
a difference in the world.

00:12:21.900 --> 00:12:26.180
You can build brighter futures
for a generation of children.

00:12:26.180 --> 00:12:28.020
So once again, thank you very much

00:12:28.020 --> 00:12:29.973
and back to you, Saad and Linda.

00:12:30.960 --> 00:12:34.740
- Thanks, so I'll start
with the questions.

00:12:34.740 --> 00:12:39.620
The, obviously there has
been a lot of disruption

00:12:39.620 --> 00:12:41.950
of routine services, as you mentioned

00:12:43.556 --> 00:12:46.110
and including immunization services

00:12:46.110 --> 00:12:51.110
and UNICEF along with
it's in-country partners

00:12:52.570 --> 00:12:54.660
often forms the backbone

00:12:54.660 --> 00:12:59.240
of the technical capacity
that various countries have.

00:12:59.240 --> 00:13:01.410
What is your assessment
of the current situation?

00:13:01.410 --> 00:13:04.194
Has, do you think there
has been a bounce-back

00:13:04.194 --> 00:13:06.280
in immunization services?

00:13:06.280 --> 00:13:07.113
Do you think,

00:13:07.113 --> 00:13:09.170
I know that you issued a call

00:13:09.170 --> 00:13:12.140
for resumption of these services lately,

00:13:12.140 --> 00:13:14.470
so if in terms of your expectations

00:13:14.470 --> 00:13:18.513
whether the system is
likely to bounce back soon?

00:13:21.140 --> 00:13:26.050
- So the honest answer
is we don't know yet.

00:13:26.050 --> 00:13:27.760
What has happened is

00:13:27.760 --> 00:13:31.920
that primary healthcare services are down.

00:13:31.920 --> 00:13:34.216
So routine immunizations,

00:13:34.216 --> 00:13:38.210
those routine immunizations that you get

00:13:38.210 --> 00:13:40.170
when you are less than one year old

00:13:40.170 --> 00:13:43.390
or less than five years old are way down.

00:13:43.390 --> 00:13:48.357
In some of our countries,
they are down 40 to 60%.

00:13:48.357 --> 00:13:52.996
And some of that is due to
supplies being interrupted.

00:13:52.996 --> 00:13:57.996
It has been difficult to find
air freight, cargo space,

00:13:58.370 --> 00:14:01.280
it has been difficult
sometimes to cross the airspace

00:14:01.280 --> 00:14:04.118
sometimes to cross into the country,

00:14:04.118 --> 00:14:06.380
to allow for delivery.

00:14:06.380 --> 00:14:07.330
At other times

00:14:07.330 --> 00:14:10.500
it is because a family is worried

00:14:10.500 --> 00:14:12.750
that if they go to a
primary health clinic,

00:14:12.750 --> 00:14:16.990
that they will get COVID-19
or another disease,

00:14:16.990 --> 00:14:19.240
and they are just worried.

00:14:19.240 --> 00:14:22.870
So routine immunizations are down.

00:14:22.870 --> 00:14:26.190
And as you know, they
have to be in a pattern

00:14:26.190 --> 00:14:30.610
and we are very worried that
families are not coming back

00:14:30.610 --> 00:14:33.550
in the same numbers that
they should be coming back.

00:14:33.550 --> 00:14:35.170
And we're worried about measles.

00:14:35.170 --> 00:14:36.733
We're worried about polio.

00:14:37.780 --> 00:14:40.240
Then we also have campaigns

00:14:40.240 --> 00:14:44.520
and campaigns really, such
as the polio campaign,

00:14:44.520 --> 00:14:48.810
they become a big push and it
is backed by the government.

00:14:48.810 --> 00:14:51.710
They want to make sure that they vaccinate

00:14:51.710 --> 00:14:53.190
a hundred percent of the children

00:14:53.190 --> 00:14:55.250
or as close as we can get to it.

00:14:55.250 --> 00:14:59.860
And we go by field area within a country.

00:14:59.860 --> 00:15:03.580
These campaigns are more likely
to be coming back quickly

00:15:03.580 --> 00:15:06.840
and Saad, I can tell you
that they are coming back.

00:15:06.840 --> 00:15:11.830
So the specialty campaigns
are starting to come back

00:15:11.830 --> 00:15:13.540
into the system again.

00:15:13.540 --> 00:15:15.851
But we have one other real problem

00:15:15.851 --> 00:15:19.496
which is that there is
a lot on social media

00:15:19.496 --> 00:15:24.496
that are negative commentaries

00:15:24.670 --> 00:15:26.510
about getting a vaccination,

00:15:26.510 --> 00:15:28.200
that if you do vaccinate

00:15:28.200 --> 00:15:31.380
that your children will get
whatever that disease is

00:15:31.380 --> 00:15:33.240
that you're being vaccinated from.

00:15:33.240 --> 00:15:36.793
We have got to be able to
counter the misinformation

00:15:36.793 --> 00:15:41.280
that is often too ubiquitous

00:15:41.280 --> 00:15:44.695
on the internet and in
social media platforms

00:15:44.695 --> 00:15:47.970
that say that you should worry

00:15:47.970 --> 00:15:50.690
that you should have vaccine hesitancy.

00:15:50.690 --> 00:15:54.350
We need to be able to convince
people that vaccines work.

00:15:54.350 --> 00:15:55.220
They do work.

00:15:55.220 --> 00:15:58.600
We know they work, but we
need families to believe it.

00:15:58.600 --> 00:16:02.300
And to come in and to get
their children vaccinated.

00:16:02.300 --> 00:16:05.040
It's also gonna be true
for adults in COVID-19.

00:16:05.040 --> 00:16:10.040
So Saad, any ideas that
you and the school have

00:16:10.800 --> 00:16:14.138
on ways that we can talk
about getting vaccinations,

00:16:14.138 --> 00:16:15.863
this would be very important.

00:16:16.740 --> 00:16:17.697
- Yeah, well, thank you.

00:16:17.697 --> 00:16:19.640
And in fact, as you alluded to,

00:16:19.640 --> 00:16:23.120
we are starting and have
started a partnership

00:16:23.120 --> 00:16:27.330
with UNICEF on specifically
on addressing misinformation

00:16:27.330 --> 00:16:29.900
and in certain cases,
unfortunately disinformation,

00:16:29.900 --> 00:16:33.840
but using evidence-based
approaches to do so

00:16:33.840 --> 00:16:36.170
because, you know, just like we need

00:16:36.170 --> 00:16:40.590
to bring the best science
to vaccine development,

00:16:40.590 --> 00:16:42.720
we need to bring the best set of evidence

00:16:42.720 --> 00:16:46.050
from social sciences and
other quantitative sciences

00:16:46.050 --> 00:16:47.295
and other sciences

00:16:47.295 --> 00:16:52.190
to addressing misinformation
and disinformation.

00:16:52.190 --> 00:16:54.563
I'll give the next question to Linda.

00:16:57.860 --> 00:16:59.410
- Many of us are concerned that

00:16:59.410 --> 00:17:02.240
during the pandemic, there
has been a disparate effect

00:17:02.240 --> 00:17:05.020
of COVID related restrictions and systems

00:17:05.020 --> 00:17:06.680
and services disruptions

00:17:06.680 --> 00:17:08.680
on children who are living in poverty

00:17:08.680 --> 00:17:12.270
and members of other at-risk
or marginalized populations.

00:17:12.270 --> 00:17:13.540
And we've all heard reports

00:17:13.540 --> 00:17:16.510
of increased food insecurity,
domestic violence,

00:17:16.510 --> 00:17:20.180
sexual abuse, child,
marriage, and child labor.

00:17:20.180 --> 00:17:22.630
What kinds of things are
you seeing in the field?

00:17:24.720 --> 00:17:27.400
- Well, I wish I could tell
you that we were not seeing

00:17:27.400 --> 00:17:29.030
any of those things, but we are,

00:17:29.030 --> 00:17:30.820
we're seeing all of them.

00:17:30.820 --> 00:17:33.010
So one of the things that happens

00:17:33.010 --> 00:17:38.010
in COVID-19 is that resources
and services are rearranged.

00:17:38.937 --> 00:17:41.854
(muffled speaking)

00:17:44.190 --> 00:17:46.760
And what it means is that some
of your healthcare workers

00:17:46.760 --> 00:17:49.450
who had been working on another area

00:17:49.450 --> 00:17:53.910
are now redirected to working on COVID.

00:17:53.910 --> 00:17:57.880
So part of what we are very worried about

00:17:57.880 --> 00:18:00.420
are the noncommunicable diseases.

00:18:00.420 --> 00:18:04.000
And you mentioned things
like child marriage

00:18:04.000 --> 00:18:07.050
and you mentioned some areas

00:18:07.050 --> 00:18:08.940
that we often don't speak about,

00:18:08.940 --> 00:18:11.500
which is the mental health problems

00:18:11.500 --> 00:18:13.450
and these all need counseling

00:18:13.450 --> 00:18:15.730
and they need psychosocial support

00:18:15.730 --> 00:18:18.750
so that when our workforces

00:18:18.750 --> 00:18:21.620
out around the world are
diverted into something else

00:18:21.620 --> 00:18:25.420
because these are usually
very thin capacities

00:18:25.420 --> 00:18:28.380
but when they are now battling COVID-19

00:18:28.380 --> 00:18:31.680
then the support in other areas drops off.

00:18:31.680 --> 00:18:33.270
So the needs are great.

00:18:33.270 --> 00:18:35.840
It is true in almost every country.

00:18:35.840 --> 00:18:39.590
We worry the most to your very good point

00:18:39.590 --> 00:18:42.450
about the poorest countries.

00:18:42.450 --> 00:18:45.320
We worry about the poorest populations

00:18:45.320 --> 00:18:46.730
in the poorest countries.

00:18:46.730 --> 00:18:49.900
And we particularly
worried about the girls.

00:18:49.900 --> 00:18:53.930
So girls become liable
for early child marriage.

00:18:53.930 --> 00:18:57.630
It is often because their
parents wish to keep them safe.

00:18:57.630 --> 00:19:00.260
And they believe that
when they reach puberty

00:19:00.260 --> 00:19:03.080
that to keep them safe means
that they should marry them.

00:19:03.080 --> 00:19:07.390
They do not have the economic
wherewithal to keep them,

00:19:07.390 --> 00:19:09.380
and that they worry that going to school

00:19:09.380 --> 00:19:10.990
will not be helpful.

00:19:10.990 --> 00:19:13.280
So child marriage looms very large

00:19:13.280 --> 00:19:16.473
for girls on many continents.

00:19:17.400 --> 00:19:20.800
We also worry about just household work.

00:19:20.800 --> 00:19:23.130
Girls being kept out of secondary school.

00:19:23.130 --> 00:19:25.210
You know, the numbers,

00:19:25.210 --> 00:19:29.480
primary school almost all
children have a chance

00:19:29.480 --> 00:19:31.220
to go to primary school

00:19:31.220 --> 00:19:34.220
but then the numbers drop
way off for secondary school.

00:19:34.220 --> 00:19:36.098
And for girls that usually is,

00:19:36.098 --> 00:19:39.470
staying in the household, look
after older family members

00:19:39.470 --> 00:19:40.880
or child marriage.

00:19:40.880 --> 00:19:44.700
For boys, it is usually
that they are getting lured

00:19:44.700 --> 00:19:47.603
into some form of a service to a militia,

00:19:48.780 --> 00:19:52.320
or that they are asked to make
a living out on the streets

00:19:52.320 --> 00:19:53.890
to earn money for their family.

00:19:53.890 --> 00:19:58.670
And with the economic crisis
that has come with COVID-19,

00:19:58.670 --> 00:20:02.302
if your father or mother are without work,

00:20:02.302 --> 00:20:03.650
you go out to work

00:20:03.650 --> 00:20:05.730
as a young man or as a young woman.

00:20:05.730 --> 00:20:08.150
And we are very worried

00:20:08.150 --> 00:20:09.800
that they will never return to school.

00:20:09.800 --> 00:20:12.830
And that's what we saw
during the Ebola crisis

00:20:12.830 --> 00:20:17.400
is that at least half of the
girls did not return to school.

00:20:17.400 --> 00:20:19.710
So it's important.

00:20:19.710 --> 00:20:22.290
We need to be thinking about the children

00:20:22.290 --> 00:20:24.810
that are most vulnerable

00:20:24.810 --> 00:20:27.100
and we need to think
about the reallocation

00:20:27.100 --> 00:20:29.610
of resources within a country

00:20:29.610 --> 00:20:32.210
so that you can invest in
children and young people.

00:20:34.110 --> 00:20:36.950
- I know that you talked
a bit earlier about

00:20:36.950 --> 00:20:39.250
challenges and difficulties in getting

00:20:39.250 --> 00:20:40.880
food and nutrition to children

00:20:40.880 --> 00:20:42.500
in sort of far off places,

00:20:42.500 --> 00:20:44.620
or really anywhere during the pandemic.

00:20:44.620 --> 00:20:46.110
Have there been strategies that

00:20:46.110 --> 00:20:48.293
have been successful kind of?

00:20:49.870 --> 00:20:51.980
- So one of the things we dream about

00:20:51.980 --> 00:20:55.020
is to really look at systems.

00:20:55.020 --> 00:20:57.480
So food systems operate

00:20:57.480 --> 00:21:00.910
from a rural farm to an urban center,

00:21:00.910 --> 00:21:02.960
and you know the numbers,

00:21:02.960 --> 00:21:07.840
urbanization is moving
along at a very fast pace.

00:21:07.840 --> 00:21:10.290
And what it means is the diets change.

00:21:10.290 --> 00:21:11.460
There's a different diet

00:21:11.460 --> 00:21:13.753
when you are living on your own farm,

00:21:13.753 --> 00:21:17.140
than there is when you
go into an urban center.

00:21:17.140 --> 00:21:21.020
But food systems need
to follow the population

00:21:21.020 --> 00:21:25.130
and they need to provide a diverse diet.

00:21:25.130 --> 00:21:27.040
We believe that at least

00:21:27.040 --> 00:21:29.900
one out of every three children

00:21:29.900 --> 00:21:32.460
are not getting the
nutrition that they need.

00:21:32.460 --> 00:21:33.750
Two are not,

00:21:33.750 --> 00:21:37.090
two out of three are not
getting a diverse diet.

00:21:37.090 --> 00:21:38.350
So what does that mean?

00:21:38.350 --> 00:21:40.870
Either that it's not affordable

00:21:40.870 --> 00:21:43.290
or that it's not accessible

00:21:43.290 --> 00:21:46.920
or that their parents or they do not know

00:21:46.920 --> 00:21:50.210
what would comprise a nutritious diet.

00:21:50.210 --> 00:21:52.670
but it has long lasting effects,

00:21:52.670 --> 00:21:56.210
so if a child is not properly nourished

00:21:56.210 --> 00:21:59.200
in that first three years of life

00:21:59.200 --> 00:22:01.170
that first thousand days,

00:22:01.170 --> 00:22:05.450
it just means that their
bones do not grow as strong

00:22:05.450 --> 00:22:09.740
that their brains do not
grow as well connected

00:22:09.740 --> 00:22:13.460
as children, who've had a
better chance at nutrition.

00:22:13.460 --> 00:22:17.880
And if we are to ever
catch stunting or wasting

00:22:17.880 --> 00:22:19.780
and stop them in their tracks

00:22:19.780 --> 00:22:24.240
we are going to have to get
these food systems to work.

00:22:24.240 --> 00:22:27.540
So food systems are extremely important.

00:22:27.540 --> 00:22:31.390
And then there's one other,
very sobering statistic.

00:22:31.390 --> 00:22:34.150
Young people don't want
to be farmers anymore.

00:22:34.150 --> 00:22:39.150
They see their parents just
struggling to make it on a farm

00:22:39.290 --> 00:22:40.540
and they leave the farms,

00:22:40.540 --> 00:22:42.010
they go to the cities

00:22:42.010 --> 00:22:44.010
where there's digital connectivity

00:22:44.010 --> 00:22:47.080
they're adolescents, and it's very hard.

00:22:47.080 --> 00:22:50.060
So we're going to need lots of farmers.

00:22:50.060 --> 00:22:52.140
They're gonna have to
be digitally connected.

00:22:52.140 --> 00:22:54.970
But young people are gonna
have to want to be farmers

00:22:54.970 --> 00:22:58.100
or the food systems cannot work well.

00:22:58.100 --> 00:23:01.720
And during COVID-19, we've
seen many border closures.

00:23:01.720 --> 00:23:04.480
So food systems were stopped often

00:23:04.480 --> 00:23:06.380
but we need governments to help us.

00:23:06.380 --> 00:23:08.210
We need businesses to help us.

00:23:08.210 --> 00:23:10.750
We need schools to help us to train

00:23:10.750 --> 00:23:13.840
but we need above all good food systems.

00:23:13.840 --> 00:23:17.440
And the realization that
nutrition is important

00:23:17.440 --> 00:23:20.000
not just for small children

00:23:20.000 --> 00:23:21.780
but also for adolescent mothers

00:23:21.780 --> 00:23:24.023
for everyone in every stage of life.

00:23:26.370 --> 00:23:30.660
- Great, so there is, you know

00:23:30.660 --> 00:23:32.163
in this outbreak,

00:23:33.630 --> 00:23:37.610
it has been an all hands on deck situation

00:23:37.610 --> 00:23:39.780
and where everyone has to come together

00:23:39.780 --> 00:23:42.300
at the country level
and at the global level.

00:23:42.300 --> 00:23:45.860
And UNICEF has provided
some of the most useful

00:23:45.860 --> 00:23:49.200
pair of hands in this global effort.

00:23:49.200 --> 00:23:50.610
But the reality is

00:23:50.610 --> 00:23:54.400
that this outbreak is not
just impacting children

00:23:54.400 --> 00:23:56.290
it's impacting adults

00:23:56.290 --> 00:23:59.220
particularly, but not
exclusively the elderly.

00:23:59.220 --> 00:24:04.220
And UNICEF's stated mission
focuses on children.

00:24:04.880 --> 00:24:08.040
And so how do you sort of resolve that

00:24:09.373 --> 00:24:10.377
not tension, you know,

00:24:10.377 --> 00:24:14.520
that alignment or how do
you deal with that alignment

00:24:14.520 --> 00:24:17.360
in terms of UNICEF's child focused mission

00:24:17.360 --> 00:24:20.730
but having skills and
resources and capacity

00:24:20.730 --> 00:24:24.060
specifically at the country level

00:24:24.060 --> 00:24:26.400
that is so useful and essential

00:24:26.400 --> 00:24:29.603
for a pandemic response
that goes beyond children?

00:24:31.810 --> 00:24:35.840
- Well, so Saad, we try to
be available for everyone

00:24:35.840 --> 00:24:37.930
in our expertise and our knowledge

00:24:37.930 --> 00:24:40.520
and our country presence

00:24:40.520 --> 00:24:45.520
which is indeed a real asset
at this moment in time.

00:24:45.920 --> 00:24:49.990
So what happens when COVID-19 hits?

00:24:49.990 --> 00:24:53.290
The government often talks
about what they should be doing

00:24:53.290 --> 00:24:57.300
with their budgets and
what should they invest in.

00:24:57.300 --> 00:24:58.990
And we try to make the case

00:24:58.990 --> 00:25:01.010
and I hope we're making it well,

00:25:01.010 --> 00:25:02.810
that an investment in children

00:25:02.810 --> 00:25:04.840
is the best investment you can make.

00:25:04.840 --> 00:25:06.790
They are the ultimate investment,

00:25:06.790 --> 00:25:08.186
as they often say

00:25:08.186 --> 00:25:12.930
we're 25% of the world, but
we're 100% of your future.

00:25:12.930 --> 00:25:15.500
So we try to convince governments

00:25:15.500 --> 00:25:17.300
that they need to make an investment

00:25:18.700 --> 00:25:23.100
in education and health systems
primarily, but for children.

00:25:23.100 --> 00:25:24.023
The second is

00:25:24.023 --> 00:25:27.160
that the expertise that we carry often

00:25:27.160 --> 00:25:29.870
follows in a child's life.

00:25:29.870 --> 00:25:32.370
So for instance

00:25:32.370 --> 00:25:35.250
mental health is something
that we have not talked

00:25:35.250 --> 00:25:38.620
about in our age group,

00:25:38.620 --> 00:25:40.340
because we were afraid to do so.

00:25:40.340 --> 00:25:42.020
You didn't tell your family,

00:25:42.020 --> 00:25:44.590
you certainly didn't
tell your boss at work,

00:25:44.590 --> 00:25:46.430
or you might lose your job,

00:25:46.430 --> 00:25:49.350
but this generation of young people,

00:25:49.350 --> 00:25:51.230
they want to talk about it.

00:25:51.230 --> 00:25:53.780
We have to make it okay.

00:25:53.780 --> 00:25:56.500
So what UNICEF can do in a country

00:25:56.500 --> 00:25:59.910
is talk about mental health
and how important it is.

00:25:59.910 --> 00:26:03.430
In most mental health problems
start by the age of 14.

00:26:03.430 --> 00:26:06.870
So if we do not catch
it early, in childhood,

00:26:06.870 --> 00:26:08.810
it means that there will be repercussions

00:26:08.810 --> 00:26:10.470
in the decades to come

00:26:10.470 --> 00:26:12.540
in the adult population.

00:26:12.540 --> 00:26:15.980
And the lockdown and the
restrictions in movement

00:26:15.980 --> 00:26:20.980
have created a lot of violence,
physical and sexual at home.

00:26:21.660 --> 00:26:26.660
It has also created a new
dynamic in many households

00:26:27.730 --> 00:26:30.940
because one or another of the breadwinners

00:26:30.940 --> 00:26:33.650
has lost their economic income.

00:26:33.650 --> 00:26:36.280
It creates tension and violence

00:26:36.280 --> 00:26:39.640
in a way that we have not seen.

00:26:39.640 --> 00:26:44.640
So helplines, parenting forums
are all extremely important.

00:26:44.780 --> 00:26:47.200
So you might think

00:26:47.200 --> 00:26:50.220
that that was just because
you UNICEF knew children

00:26:50.220 --> 00:26:53.350
but it's because children
live in families.

00:26:53.350 --> 00:26:55.276
And we look after the families.

00:26:55.276 --> 00:26:59.230
And for UNICEF it will be important

00:26:59.230 --> 00:27:03.090
to look after mental health,
to keep families together.

00:27:03.090 --> 00:27:06.510
It will be important to look
after water and water system

00:27:06.510 --> 00:27:10.710
to try to keep communities
safe and clean, hygienic.

00:27:10.710 --> 00:27:13.620
It will be important to
look after distance learning

00:27:13.620 --> 00:27:15.800
because children need to be in school.

00:27:15.800 --> 00:27:17.390
It's their futures.

00:27:17.390 --> 00:27:20.440
And we've got to focus on immunization

00:27:20.440 --> 00:27:23.230
because children can lose their lives

00:27:23.230 --> 00:27:27.810
to measles or another
disease rather than to COVID.

00:27:27.810 --> 00:27:29.740
And then to Linda's earlier point

00:27:29.740 --> 00:27:31.570
we must be able to make sure

00:27:31.570 --> 00:27:33.680
that there's enough nutrition and food.

00:27:33.680 --> 00:27:37.167
So the children can move
beyond this COVID time

00:27:37.167 --> 00:27:40.023
and they still have
healthy, strong bodies.

00:27:42.210 --> 00:27:46.040
- I, thank you for even alluding to

00:27:46.040 --> 00:27:48.680
just the numerous needs that children have

00:27:48.680 --> 00:27:52.850
and how those early and
foundational childhood experiences

00:27:52.850 --> 00:27:56.855
and health status can sort
of have lifelong effects.

00:27:56.855 --> 00:27:59.640
One of the things that we as
pediatricians always stress

00:27:59.640 --> 00:28:02.670
is the importance of play
towards normal development.

00:28:02.670 --> 00:28:05.620
And at a time when everyone
is incredibly stressed out

00:28:05.620 --> 00:28:08.040
when you know, there's
a lot of tension around

00:28:08.040 --> 00:28:10.510
children are out of school,
taking on other roles,

00:28:10.510 --> 00:28:13.510
what can be done at the
country level and globally

00:28:13.510 --> 00:28:16.866
to promote the importance of
just regular childhood play

00:28:16.866 --> 00:28:19.100
and routine physical activity

00:28:19.100 --> 00:28:20.660
in terms of children's wellbeing

00:28:20.660 --> 00:28:22.343
and normal childhood development.

00:28:24.380 --> 00:28:25.213
- Thank you Linda.

00:28:25.213 --> 00:28:26.780
Well we do believe in play

00:28:26.780 --> 00:28:28.560
and I hope you're going to allow

00:28:28.560 --> 00:28:30.770
some of these students that are still

00:28:30.770 --> 00:28:33.520
around the Yale center time to play also

00:28:33.520 --> 00:28:35.433
because we never grow out of it.

00:28:36.580 --> 00:28:38.870
But during the height of the pandemic,

00:28:38.870 --> 00:28:42.840
when there were many lockdown restrictions

00:28:42.840 --> 00:28:45.000
that you must stay in your apartments,

00:28:45.000 --> 00:28:48.850
and this is in many of the
highly developed cities

00:28:48.850 --> 00:28:51.360
in Europe or in North America,

00:28:51.360 --> 00:28:52.860
it was very hard on children

00:28:52.860 --> 00:28:56.150
because they want to go play

00:28:56.150 --> 00:28:58.280
and you can't necessarily play

00:28:58.280 --> 00:29:00.110
in the apartment that you're living in.

00:29:00.110 --> 00:29:02.890
And there, all of a sudden,
the apartments seem very small

00:29:02.890 --> 00:29:05.980
and very crowded, and you
didn't have any friends there.

00:29:05.980 --> 00:29:09.030
And so it's very hard on a child.

00:29:09.030 --> 00:29:10.609
So we were encouraging

00:29:10.609 --> 00:29:12.730
that there'd be times every day

00:29:12.730 --> 00:29:14.340
when children could go out and play

00:29:14.340 --> 00:29:17.040
and they could go out and
play just right around

00:29:17.040 --> 00:29:18.520
their apartment building

00:29:18.520 --> 00:29:20.380
or that there might be a nearby park

00:29:20.380 --> 00:29:22.440
that they could go to safely

00:29:22.440 --> 00:29:24.890
at a distance and they could go play.

00:29:24.890 --> 00:29:27.620
But it's very important,
it's part of the makeup

00:29:27.620 --> 00:29:32.620
of a childhood and of
a healthy young adult.

00:29:34.900 --> 00:29:37.550
So it's a very important issue.

00:29:37.550 --> 00:29:38.909
We've been speaking up about it,

00:29:38.909 --> 00:29:42.980
but we can use the help
of every pediatrician

00:29:44.001 --> 00:29:48.253
who can prescribe it to families
and to governments alike.

00:29:50.510 --> 00:29:52.440
- Very nice, so we have a question

00:29:52.440 --> 00:29:57.440
from our students compiled
by one of our faculty members

00:29:57.740 --> 00:30:00.103
who teachers our global health course.

00:30:01.150 --> 00:30:03.630
And so a number of our
public health students

00:30:03.630 --> 00:30:07.230
and others are passionate

00:30:07.230 --> 00:30:09.839
about working in humanitarian sector.

00:30:09.839 --> 00:30:13.480
What are the skills,
especially skills of the future

00:30:14.540 --> 00:30:16.780
that you think they should acquire

00:30:16.780 --> 00:30:20.960
for them to be able to
serve more effectively

00:30:20.960 --> 00:30:22.640
in the humanitarian sector?

00:30:22.640 --> 00:30:24.533
Both in health and otherwise as well.

00:30:26.340 --> 00:30:28.450
- So be a problem solver

00:30:28.450 --> 00:30:31.040
I don't think we can teach
you everything in school,

00:30:31.040 --> 00:30:33.400
and we can't teach you
everything with books,

00:30:33.400 --> 00:30:37.270
but you will learn from
the context you're in

00:30:37.270 --> 00:30:40.130
the beauty of the world around us

00:30:40.130 --> 00:30:44.700
is that we have thousands
upon thousands of cultures

00:30:44.700 --> 00:30:46.410
and they will all react differently.

00:30:46.410 --> 00:30:49.390
So there'll be different ways
that you can affect them.

00:30:49.390 --> 00:30:51.120
And when you get out into the field

00:30:51.120 --> 00:30:56.120
with an agency like UNICEF,
you will see the environment

00:30:56.180 --> 00:30:59.220
and the ecosystem that we are working in

00:30:59.220 --> 00:31:01.290
building trust with your community

00:31:01.290 --> 00:31:03.080
is one of the most important things

00:31:03.080 --> 00:31:06.190
that anyone working in
health can do or education,

00:31:06.190 --> 00:31:08.000
but in health particularly.

00:31:08.000 --> 00:31:10.565
We saw it in the Democratic
Republic of Congo,

00:31:10.565 --> 00:31:13.290
when Ebola first came in,

00:31:13.290 --> 00:31:17.770
if a community health worker
spoke the local dialects

00:31:17.770 --> 00:31:19.130
and was of the community

00:31:19.130 --> 00:31:21.849
and was considered an important
member of a community,

00:31:21.849 --> 00:31:25.590
they were listened to,
people paid attention.

00:31:25.590 --> 00:31:27.960
What you should not eat

00:31:27.960 --> 00:31:29.270
in the way of bushmeat,

00:31:29.270 --> 00:31:32.170
what you should not do in the way of

00:31:32.170 --> 00:31:35.350
preparing bodies for burial,

00:31:35.350 --> 00:31:36.830
people listened to them.

00:31:36.830 --> 00:31:40.070
So having that trust is important.

00:31:40.070 --> 00:31:41.590
And to have that trust

00:31:41.590 --> 00:31:43.540
you need to listen very closely

00:31:43.540 --> 00:31:46.150
to the communities that we are serving,

00:31:46.150 --> 00:31:48.310
that you will be serving.

00:31:48.310 --> 00:31:51.470
So I think that's probably

00:31:51.470 --> 00:31:55.130
the single most important quality.

00:31:55.130 --> 00:31:57.250
Maybe you could call it empathy.

00:31:57.250 --> 00:32:00.210
Maybe you could call it good listening,

00:32:00.210 --> 00:32:02.700
but you need to feel what the people

00:32:02.700 --> 00:32:04.530
you are serving are feeling.

00:32:04.530 --> 00:32:07.360
And to see life through their eyes.

00:32:07.360 --> 00:32:10.820
And if you can, you can help them.

00:32:10.820 --> 00:32:15.410
The other thing that I
can see in our world is

00:32:15.410 --> 00:32:18.430
it is going to be
essential that we move on

00:32:18.430 --> 00:32:22.990
primary health care and that
we move in digital health.

00:32:22.990 --> 00:32:25.540
So understanding digital health

00:32:25.540 --> 00:32:28.087
and what its capacities can be

00:32:28.087 --> 00:32:33.087
in high tech, low tech,
no tech environments

00:32:33.920 --> 00:32:34.973
will be very important.

00:32:34.973 --> 00:32:37.970
I mean, right now we move vaccines

00:32:37.970 --> 00:32:41.060
to little refrigerators out
in the rural communities

00:32:41.060 --> 00:32:43.970
that have one little solar panel

00:32:43.970 --> 00:32:46.763
that keeps them running day and night.

00:32:47.930 --> 00:32:52.509
That is one kind of a low tech solution

00:32:52.509 --> 00:32:56.600
that's affordable, that we can
put everywhere in the world.

00:32:56.600 --> 00:33:01.600
And you need to know all
of those kinds of solutions

00:33:02.230 --> 00:33:04.325
because digital health is coming.

00:33:04.325 --> 00:33:07.290
Artificial intelligence is coming also

00:33:07.290 --> 00:33:10.610
so that you can remotely diagnose,

00:33:10.610 --> 00:33:12.550
and you can do it over a cell phone,

00:33:12.550 --> 00:33:14.500
but we don't have it in every place.

00:33:14.500 --> 00:33:16.800
And we are trying to connect

00:33:16.800 --> 00:33:19.780
the other half of the
world to the internet.

00:33:19.780 --> 00:33:22.260
We are trying to connect
every school in the world

00:33:22.260 --> 00:33:25.220
to the internet in the next five years,

00:33:25.220 --> 00:33:26.370
if we can do it.

00:33:26.370 --> 00:33:27.280
And if we could

00:33:27.280 --> 00:33:29.670
then it means every
community would be connected

00:33:29.670 --> 00:33:32.170
and every primary health clinic.

00:33:32.170 --> 00:33:34.700
So those would be good skills to learn,

00:33:34.700 --> 00:33:39.700
in addition to that very
important but difficult one

00:33:40.150 --> 00:33:41.880
that's empathy of just listening

00:33:41.880 --> 00:33:44.423
and feeling that you
are with your community.

00:33:47.880 --> 00:33:52.880
- Linda, sorry, you're on mute.

00:33:56.188 --> 00:33:57.650
- Sorry, I knew that.

00:33:57.650 --> 00:33:58.950
Maybe we'll do one more,

00:33:58.950 --> 00:34:01.733
one more of the questions
from the Q and A,

00:34:01.733 --> 00:34:04.350
Someone has asked about concerns

00:34:04.350 --> 00:34:05.610
in working with adolescents

00:34:05.610 --> 00:34:07.560
on sexual and reproductive health

00:34:07.560 --> 00:34:10.860
about how can we reassure both
adolescents and their parents

00:34:10.860 --> 00:34:13.800
that it's safe to return
to services and care?

00:34:13.800 --> 00:34:15.700
And if there are any insights to be gained

00:34:15.700 --> 00:34:18.530
from previous emergencies like Ebola?

00:34:18.530 --> 00:34:21.940
And I think that also ties
into what we've talked about

00:34:21.940 --> 00:34:24.080
increased domestic violence,
child marriage, you know

00:34:24.080 --> 00:34:26.980
an access to just reproductive
health services in general.

00:34:29.000 --> 00:34:34.000
- So in the news today was
one of those wonderful stories

00:34:34.810 --> 00:34:39.810
about a hospital in which a
number of mothers had come

00:34:40.850 --> 00:34:42.560
because they would accept mothers

00:34:42.560 --> 00:34:45.040
who tested positive in COVID

00:34:45.040 --> 00:34:48.830
and they were coming there
to deliver their babies

00:34:48.830 --> 00:34:51.680
and this hospital accepted them.

00:34:51.680 --> 00:34:54.370
So they were getting a good experience

00:34:54.370 --> 00:34:56.831
on how to deliver healthy babies

00:34:56.831 --> 00:34:59.832
that are not testing positive on COVID

00:34:59.832 --> 00:35:04.832
from positively tested COVID mothers.

00:35:06.480 --> 00:35:10.380
So an experience from
one hospital like that

00:35:10.380 --> 00:35:12.520
is a very important experience.

00:35:12.520 --> 00:35:16.000
And it's one that we need to pass along

00:35:16.000 --> 00:35:17.800
to a number of other countries

00:35:17.800 --> 00:35:20.710
and villages and towns and cities,

00:35:20.710 --> 00:35:25.710
because everyone needs that prenatal care.

00:35:26.680 --> 00:35:28.172
And you need the care

00:35:28.172 --> 00:35:32.570
before, during and after
the birth of a child.

00:35:32.570 --> 00:35:35.430
So obstetric care is way down.

00:35:35.430 --> 00:35:37.540
I mean, I wish that
people were coming into

00:35:37.540 --> 00:35:39.430
the primary health clinics

00:35:39.430 --> 00:35:41.210
and into the community centers,

00:35:41.210 --> 00:35:43.120
but they're staying home.

00:35:43.120 --> 00:35:45.440
So that then means that

00:35:45.440 --> 00:35:48.490
midwives who are out in the villages

00:35:48.490 --> 00:35:50.480
are exceedingly important.

00:35:50.480 --> 00:35:54.460
And it means that they need water and soap

00:35:54.460 --> 00:35:57.460
so that they can try to keep everything

00:35:57.460 --> 00:36:00.120
as hygienic as possible.

00:36:00.120 --> 00:36:04.133
The second example is from HIV AIDS.

00:36:05.050 --> 00:36:09.180
So there are many young people,
particularly adolescents

00:36:09.180 --> 00:36:12.470
who want to test themselves for HIV AIDS.

00:36:12.470 --> 00:36:15.050
They also want to test
themselves for COVID.

00:36:15.050 --> 00:36:17.560
They want to be self-empowered.

00:36:17.560 --> 00:36:20.613
And so they want a self test kit.

00:36:21.450 --> 00:36:24.060
And we're not very good as a world

00:36:24.060 --> 00:36:25.730
in developing self tests,

00:36:25.730 --> 00:36:27.350
we tend to develop tests

00:36:27.350 --> 00:36:30.770
that a doctor gives to us
or a nurse gives to us,

00:36:30.770 --> 00:36:33.370
and then that's enough.

00:36:33.370 --> 00:36:36.020
But young people want that.

00:36:36.020 --> 00:36:38.620
And they want to tell each other

00:36:38.620 --> 00:36:41.290
about how to keep themselves safe.

00:36:41.290 --> 00:36:44.060
So one of the areas we've been encouraging

00:36:44.060 --> 00:36:47.260
is the young people talking
to other young people.

00:36:47.260 --> 00:36:49.600
There are young people who have decided

00:36:49.600 --> 00:36:54.220
to open up HIV AIDS clinics,
and they're operating them.

00:36:54.220 --> 00:36:57.930
They're all 17, 18, 19,
20, 21, 22 years old,

00:36:57.930 --> 00:36:59.590
and they're operating the clinics.

00:36:59.590 --> 00:37:01.480
It's not an adults clinic

00:37:01.480 --> 00:37:05.760
it's for adolescents, for
adolescents by adolescents

00:37:05.760 --> 00:37:07.960
and they really believe in it.

00:37:07.960 --> 00:37:11.150
And young people tend
to flock to those places

00:37:11.150 --> 00:37:14.850
because they trust their age mates

00:37:14.850 --> 00:37:17.700
sometimes more than they trust us.

00:37:17.700 --> 00:37:20.160
So I think there are lots of ways

00:37:20.160 --> 00:37:25.160
to get health advice, health
prevention, health treatment,

00:37:25.410 --> 00:37:29.200
out to adolescents and
at least some of the

00:37:29.200 --> 00:37:32.460
lesser serviced areas

00:37:32.460 --> 00:37:36.260
in ways that we just have
not thought of in the past,

00:37:36.260 --> 00:37:38.040
but the world is changing.

00:37:38.040 --> 00:37:42.820
And I think we can come out
of this stronger and smarter

00:37:42.820 --> 00:37:44.590
about how we can reach more

00:37:44.590 --> 00:37:46.333
of the millions of people in need.

00:37:48.220 --> 00:37:50.350
- No, that's great.

00:37:50.350 --> 00:37:52.730
And I think that's a
very useful perspective.

00:37:52.730 --> 00:37:55.610
We have a question, an
interesting question

00:37:55.610 --> 00:37:57.560
in the sense that, you know,

00:37:57.560 --> 00:37:59.810
it's about UN Resolution 1325,

00:37:59.810 --> 00:38:03.190
and for the audience, who's
not familiar with this.

00:38:03.190 --> 00:38:06.680
This is a resolution that reaffirms

00:38:06.680 --> 00:38:08.290
the role women have

00:38:09.150 --> 00:38:10.650
in conflict resolution,

00:38:10.650 --> 00:38:12.040
and there is proportionate impact

00:38:12.040 --> 00:38:16.130
they suffer from conflicts
and, you know, similar stuff.

00:38:16.130 --> 00:38:17.360
And it says that, you know,

00:38:17.360 --> 00:38:19.850
UN agencies should include women

00:38:20.800 --> 00:38:23.980
in security responses.

00:38:23.980 --> 00:38:25.080
The question is,

00:38:25.080 --> 00:38:29.973
pandemics are the global
health security you know,

00:38:31.250 --> 00:38:35.860
catastrophe or issue that we are facing.

00:38:35.860 --> 00:38:39.180
Any thoughts on, you know

00:38:39.180 --> 00:38:43.593
UN Resolution 1325 and the pandemic?

00:38:45.160 --> 00:38:50.020
- Well, it's right on the mark.

00:38:50.020 --> 00:38:55.020
Women can be massively
helpful in peace negotiations.

00:38:55.100 --> 00:38:58.480
So we are trying to think
through as the United Nations,

00:38:58.480 --> 00:39:03.480
how to link humanitarian,
development and peace together.

00:39:04.050 --> 00:39:06.840
As you all, well know,

00:39:06.840 --> 00:39:10.960
the women are on the frontline
of being healthcare workers.

00:39:10.960 --> 00:39:14.050
It is predominantly women.

00:39:14.050 --> 00:39:16.400
And part of the difficulty for women is

00:39:16.400 --> 00:39:18.140
that when the schools are closed

00:39:18.140 --> 00:39:21.040
and they need to be home with children,

00:39:21.040 --> 00:39:24.880
that their job is that
of working in a hospital

00:39:24.880 --> 00:39:26.620
or working in a school.

00:39:26.620 --> 00:39:28.600
And that's where they need to be.

00:39:28.600 --> 00:39:32.160
And it's hard for them to also be at home.

00:39:32.160 --> 00:39:34.670
So women are really being pulled

00:39:34.670 --> 00:39:37.090
in many directions at this point in time.

00:39:37.090 --> 00:39:40.230
But if we are all sensitive to this

00:39:40.230 --> 00:39:44.610
and give a chance for
more, home health care

00:39:44.610 --> 00:39:46.570
flexible work hours,

00:39:46.570 --> 00:39:47.920
ways that women,

00:39:47.920 --> 00:39:52.610
we can make it easier on women,
they are a powerful force.

00:39:52.610 --> 00:39:57.610
In the most recent negotiations for Yemen,

00:39:57.830 --> 00:40:00.280
there is a group of women

00:40:00.280 --> 00:40:03.250
who have been activated

00:40:03.250 --> 00:40:05.900
to be part of the peace negotiations.

00:40:05.900 --> 00:40:07.490
We are also seeing that now

00:40:07.490 --> 00:40:09.720
in some of the countries in the Sahil,

00:40:09.720 --> 00:40:12.340
we've been seeing in a number
of places around the world

00:40:12.340 --> 00:40:15.387
but this is a force
that is just beginning.

00:40:15.387 --> 00:40:17.410
It's very promising.

00:40:17.410 --> 00:40:21.010
We all have to get right behind
it and try to encourage it.

00:40:21.010 --> 00:40:23.540
And we need to be thoughtful

00:40:23.540 --> 00:40:28.540
about the lives of women that
we are giving them enough time

00:40:28.630 --> 00:40:33.630
and space that they can look
after their responsibilities

00:40:34.730 --> 00:40:37.460
at home as well as their responsibilities

00:40:37.460 --> 00:40:38.503
in their profession.

00:40:41.000 --> 00:40:43.313
- Very nice, Linda, do you wanna go next?

00:40:44.780 --> 00:40:47.980
- Sure, this is more of a,
kind of a broader question

00:40:47.980 --> 00:40:49.050
but I think it pulls together

00:40:49.050 --> 00:40:50.110
a lot of the different things

00:40:50.110 --> 00:40:51.600
that we've been talking about.

00:40:51.600 --> 00:40:54.780
But what are the recent
COVID related setbacks

00:40:54.780 --> 00:40:56.860
and really major barriers now

00:40:56.860 --> 00:40:59.330
to achieving the sustainable
development goals

00:40:59.330 --> 00:41:01.573
related to child health in particular?

00:41:03.810 --> 00:41:07.140
- Well, gosh, it's hard to
know where to start on that.

00:41:07.140 --> 00:41:11.160
I mean, we're very worried
that we've gone back 30 years.

00:41:11.160 --> 00:41:14.300
I mean, as a world, we
have really done well

00:41:14.300 --> 00:41:16.330
on a lot of the indicators for children

00:41:16.330 --> 00:41:19.610
like child survival and
stunting and wasting.

00:41:19.610 --> 00:41:23.200
But now we are very worried.

00:41:23.200 --> 00:41:28.200
We just do not know if
we can hold the gains.

00:41:29.370 --> 00:41:32.140
And in fact, it looks like
we cannot hold the gains.

00:41:32.140 --> 00:41:34.569
So what can we do about that?

00:41:34.569 --> 00:41:38.590
In my mind, it is that
we need to be innovative.

00:41:38.590 --> 00:41:41.110
There are so many innovations

00:41:41.110 --> 00:41:45.060
that our world dreams
of and puts into effect.

00:41:45.060 --> 00:41:47.740
I mean, when you walk
through a refugee camp

00:41:47.740 --> 00:41:51.290
you will see some very
creative ways to filter water.

00:41:51.290 --> 00:41:54.950
And they're using often, a mask

00:41:54.950 --> 00:41:56.850
or a little bit of their tent

00:41:56.850 --> 00:42:01.850
and they're creating a fiber
filter for the water faucet.

00:42:02.660 --> 00:42:05.750
That's just not far outside their door

00:42:05.750 --> 00:42:08.570
but we need to look at frugal innovations

00:42:08.570 --> 00:42:11.910
and very sophisticated
innovations and put them to work

00:42:11.910 --> 00:42:16.910
so that we can take advantage
of the new world that exists

00:42:17.330 --> 00:42:19.860
and reach more people more effectively

00:42:19.860 --> 00:42:22.730
which is why I've
mentioned digital health.

00:42:22.730 --> 00:42:27.680
It's coming, it just hasn't
come yet ubiquitously

00:42:27.680 --> 00:42:30.950
to our developing world, but it's coming.

00:42:30.950 --> 00:42:33.590
So aren't there some innovations

00:42:33.590 --> 00:42:35.880
that you have seen somewhere

00:42:35.880 --> 00:42:37.860
in some part of the world

00:42:37.860 --> 00:42:39.780
that we could be putting to use

00:42:39.780 --> 00:42:43.486
that mean that we can
leapfrog the technologies.

00:42:43.486 --> 00:42:45.270
You know, I grew up in the

00:42:46.610 --> 00:42:49.990
era when you had a
telephone that was wired

00:42:49.990 --> 00:42:51.950
into the wall at home,

00:42:51.950 --> 00:42:55.290
and it was attached

00:42:55.290 --> 00:42:58.797
you didn't move, but then cell phones came

00:42:58.797 --> 00:43:01.000
and I mean, you never look back.

00:43:01.000 --> 00:43:04.360
We need all of those in the health sector

00:43:04.360 --> 00:43:06.610
and in the education sector.

00:43:06.610 --> 00:43:09.560
One of the things that this
generation is telling us

00:43:09.560 --> 00:43:12.760
is that they care about
digital technology.

00:43:12.760 --> 00:43:14.240
They want to use it,

00:43:14.240 --> 00:43:19.020
they're empowered and they
are going to put it to use.

00:43:19.020 --> 00:43:21.170
So therefore the more that we can create

00:43:21.170 --> 00:43:23.590
an internet of good things

00:43:23.590 --> 00:43:27.120
that we can teach them
and share knowledge,

00:43:27.120 --> 00:43:29.870
we've just launched a
Global Development Commons

00:43:29.870 --> 00:43:31.930
that some students have
been helping us with

00:43:31.930 --> 00:43:35.470
from Yale and a number
of other institutions,

00:43:35.470 --> 00:43:37.600
so that you can get answers

00:43:37.600 --> 00:43:39.760
that you've been wanting to get

00:43:39.760 --> 00:43:41.860
on a Global Development Commons.

00:43:41.860 --> 00:43:46.140
And this will all be
in our internet world,

00:43:46.140 --> 00:43:50.390
but it's an exciting time

00:43:50.390 --> 00:43:52.770
for what we can change in the world.

00:43:52.770 --> 00:43:55.580
And one will be distance learning.

00:43:55.580 --> 00:43:59.060
I mean, there is no
reason why we cannot have

00:43:59.060 --> 00:44:02.400
remote learning, distance
learning in every country

00:44:02.400 --> 00:44:04.030
in the world.

00:44:04.030 --> 00:44:08.850
Whether it's by radio, television,
SMS, tablets, internet,

00:44:08.850 --> 00:44:10.610
we could do this as a world.

00:44:10.610 --> 00:44:13.525
This is one of those once in
a generation opportunities.

00:44:13.525 --> 00:44:16.190
And if we can do that for education

00:44:16.190 --> 00:44:17.463
we can do that in health.

00:44:18.970 --> 00:44:23.970
- So the next question is
one of our teen participants.

00:44:24.260 --> 00:44:27.710
So we have a few non Yale attendees.

00:44:27.710 --> 00:44:29.620
And this comes from a person

00:44:29.620 --> 00:44:32.820
who has a very sort of interesting

00:44:32.820 --> 00:44:34.830
and inspiring organization

00:44:34.830 --> 00:44:35.800
that she has set up.

00:44:35.800 --> 00:44:38.220
She's a high school student in California,

00:44:38.220 --> 00:44:42.560
she has set up this
organization for other teens

00:44:42.560 --> 00:44:47.560
to get teen adolescent
immunization rates up

00:44:47.800 --> 00:44:49.430
in various communities.

00:44:49.430 --> 00:44:51.020
And obviously we know that

00:44:51.020 --> 00:44:55.260
that age group is an
area of focus for UNICEF.

00:44:55.260 --> 00:44:56.910
Her question is

00:44:56.910 --> 00:45:01.910
that, you know, what role
could the independence

00:45:02.340 --> 00:45:07.119
of young people play in
increasing vaccination uptake?

00:45:07.119 --> 00:45:09.526
And so, some of my work
has been sort of consent

00:45:09.526 --> 00:45:12.210
in a sense of teens versus parents

00:45:12.210 --> 00:45:14.580
when these choices diverge,

00:45:14.580 --> 00:45:16.280
and for certain vaccines

00:45:17.540 --> 00:45:18.890
there is that situation

00:45:18.890 --> 00:45:22.360
when teens want it and parents don't.

00:45:22.360 --> 00:45:24.030
And sometimes vice versa.

00:45:24.030 --> 00:45:26.145
So any thoughts on that?

00:45:26.145 --> 00:45:29.760
And, you know, I know that
autonomy is a major factor

00:45:29.760 --> 00:45:31.730
in terms of working with communities

00:45:31.730 --> 00:45:32.840
but what happens.

00:45:32.840 --> 00:45:36.480
are there age specific
cutoffs where after which,

00:45:36.480 --> 00:45:39.210
even if you are a teen
that you're more empowered

00:45:39.210 --> 00:45:40.450
to make decisions, et cetera.

00:45:40.450 --> 00:45:42.463
So any, any thoughts on that dynamic?

00:45:44.310 --> 00:45:49.070
- So I think that the best way

00:45:49.070 --> 00:45:53.180
to arm a campaign is something
that you said earlier, Saad.

00:45:53.180 --> 00:45:55.860
You said, good evidence.

00:45:55.860 --> 00:45:58.390
So marshal the facts

00:45:58.390 --> 00:46:02.850
for what you know of about
the immunization campaigns

00:46:02.850 --> 00:46:07.010
for let's say, small pox, measles, polio,

00:46:07.010 --> 00:46:08.920
and then share those facts,

00:46:08.920 --> 00:46:12.140
become a trusted resource.

00:46:12.140 --> 00:46:14.780
Misinformation is very
difficult in our world,

00:46:14.780 --> 00:46:19.780
but if we can help guide our
sister and brother adolescents,

00:46:21.040 --> 00:46:25.083
to the trusted information sources

00:46:25.083 --> 00:46:28.310
they will read and learn for themselves.

00:46:28.310 --> 00:46:30.720
And it will make a difference.

00:46:30.720 --> 00:46:33.090
But Saad, if I may say

00:46:33.090 --> 00:46:37.510
it sounds like this young
lady would be very good

00:46:37.510 --> 00:46:39.340
to give us advice

00:46:39.340 --> 00:46:42.390
for what she thinks would work.

00:46:42.390 --> 00:46:47.390
I've noticed that our SMS
world is very effected

00:46:48.210 --> 00:46:50.960
if it is by someone that they know.

00:46:50.960 --> 00:46:55.540
So whether it is an age mate

00:46:55.540 --> 00:46:56.960
that they do not know,

00:46:56.960 --> 00:46:59.922
or it's an age mate who
may be as a celebrity,

00:46:59.922 --> 00:47:01.840
Millie Bobby Brown and I

00:47:01.840 --> 00:47:04.950
have done a couple of events together,

00:47:04.950 --> 00:47:07.800
but you know someone that you trust,

00:47:07.800 --> 00:47:09.820
that you would listen to

00:47:09.820 --> 00:47:14.550
that you think knows your
conditions and wants to pitch in.

00:47:14.550 --> 00:47:16.600
I think that's gonna be very important.

00:47:16.600 --> 00:47:21.410
And then Saad, if I could
just say about the future

00:47:21.410 --> 00:47:24.944
I think that we have been
working in an old model

00:47:24.944 --> 00:47:28.320
in which we thought that we adults

00:47:28.320 --> 00:47:30.440
would come up lots of solutions

00:47:30.440 --> 00:47:33.050
and that we would train and pass it on

00:47:33.050 --> 00:47:37.130
to the younger generations,
but that's not true now,

00:47:37.130 --> 00:47:39.040
the young people want to be part of it.

00:47:39.040 --> 00:47:40.750
So they want to roll up their sleeves

00:47:40.750 --> 00:47:44.570
and discover and find solutions
just right alongside us.

00:47:44.570 --> 00:47:47.070
And I think we all have to now

00:47:47.070 --> 00:47:49.633
reorient our programs to that.

00:47:50.610 --> 00:47:54.300
Let them be equal partners
in finding the solutions.

00:47:54.300 --> 00:47:59.300
So I'm ready to hear from
our activists on vaccinations

00:48:00.870 --> 00:48:02.130
to see what we can do to help.

00:48:02.130 --> 00:48:04.801
And let's start some campaigns
that will really work

00:48:04.801 --> 00:48:07.140
if they work for the young,

00:48:07.140 --> 00:48:09.400
maybe we can convince
some of the older kin

00:48:09.400 --> 00:48:10.783
to also follow suit.

00:48:12.030 --> 00:48:13.443
- Very nice, Linda.

00:48:16.800 --> 00:48:18.899
You put yourself on mute, yeah.

00:48:18.899 --> 00:48:20.030
- Yeah, got it, yeah.

00:48:20.030 --> 00:48:22.520
So this I think is a perfect followup

00:48:22.520 --> 00:48:25.650
for what you've just said,
Executive director Fore.

00:48:25.650 --> 00:48:28.410
Many of the participants
who are attending today

00:48:28.410 --> 00:48:31.210
just like many of the young
people you've talked about

00:48:31.210 --> 00:48:33.440
either are already
looking for ways to engage

00:48:33.440 --> 00:48:35.090
and will be moved to action

00:48:35.090 --> 00:48:37.060
based on what we've talked about today.

00:48:37.060 --> 00:48:39.100
And do you have any specific suggestions

00:48:39.100 --> 00:48:40.760
or even general suggestions

00:48:40.760 --> 00:48:43.610
about how people can
contribute locally and globally

00:48:43.610 --> 00:48:45.140
to improving children's health

00:48:45.140 --> 00:48:47.630
either through direct
engagement, targeted advocacy

00:48:47.630 --> 00:48:49.530
or by supporting different organizations

00:48:49.530 --> 00:48:51.030
that are working in the field?

00:48:52.680 --> 00:48:56.140
- So, Linda we talked about this earlier

00:48:56.140 --> 00:49:00.150
and you said something that is just right

00:49:00.150 --> 00:49:03.690
which is begin, start,
take the first step.

00:49:03.690 --> 00:49:06.640
So just start small start anywhere.

00:49:06.640 --> 00:49:11.320
It doesn't matter where it
can be a local food bank.

00:49:11.320 --> 00:49:14.920
It could be in a local hospital or clinic.

00:49:14.920 --> 00:49:17.370
And it doesn't matter where you are.

00:49:17.370 --> 00:49:22.370
You can tele-work to help,
or you can in-person help.

00:49:22.480 --> 00:49:25.670
And then if you are so inclined

00:49:25.670 --> 00:49:28.950
keep going in your studies,
be broad in your studies.

00:49:28.950 --> 00:49:32.770
You never know what you're
going to need in life.

00:49:32.770 --> 00:49:36.730
So try to learn as much as you can.

00:49:36.730 --> 00:49:39.510
Don't specialize too early

00:49:39.510 --> 00:49:42.800
because your world is
gonna be even more diverse

00:49:42.800 --> 00:49:46.760
than the world that Linda
and Saad and I have lived in.

00:49:46.760 --> 00:49:48.740
So learn everything you can.

00:49:48.740 --> 00:49:53.600
And then just come out here
into the field and join us.

00:49:53.600 --> 00:49:56.280
We need you, we would love to see you.

00:49:56.280 --> 00:50:01.280
And it's a big world that
has a lot of needs right now.

00:50:01.530 --> 00:50:03.010
So we need all of you,

00:50:03.010 --> 00:50:06.043
every single one of you to
come out here and help us.

00:50:07.360 --> 00:50:11.250
- Yeah, one thing, if I
may, I take the liberty

00:50:11.250 --> 00:50:16.250
to ask you to put your former
USAID administrator hat on,

00:50:17.070 --> 00:50:18.820
this is a changing,

00:50:18.820 --> 00:50:20.470
this is a season of change.

00:50:20.470 --> 00:50:22.240
And I look outside my window,

00:50:22.240 --> 00:50:24.060
there are a ton of leaves out there,

00:50:24.060 --> 00:50:27.500
and it's a moment of transition for the US

00:50:27.500 --> 00:50:28.550
in more than one way

00:50:28.550 --> 00:50:31.900
not just the weather, but otherwise.

00:50:31.900 --> 00:50:35.160
So that's irrespective of what kind of

00:50:35.160 --> 00:50:38.660
administrative of political
change is happening,

00:50:38.660 --> 00:50:42.540
these moments are a good time to reflect

00:50:42.540 --> 00:50:45.420
where US institutions should go.

00:50:45.420 --> 00:50:50.420
Especially external facing
institutions like USAID.

00:50:50.510 --> 00:50:55.493
Any thoughts on how does going forward,

00:50:56.580 --> 00:50:59.660
USAID engage with the world?

00:50:59.660 --> 00:51:02.620
Anything you would do different, you know,

00:51:02.620 --> 00:51:04.830
not based on what's
happening now, et cetera,

00:51:04.830 --> 00:51:06.360
but forward looking

00:51:06.360 --> 00:51:10.200
what would be your thoughts on, you know,

00:51:10.200 --> 00:51:15.093
USAID as the kids say a 5.0,
the new version of USAID?

00:51:17.140 --> 00:51:20.910
- USAID is an extraordinary institution

00:51:20.910 --> 00:51:25.910
with a storied past, and they
will have a storied future.

00:51:26.110 --> 00:51:30.590
So the first would be to resource it.

00:51:30.590 --> 00:51:35.590
USAID and its expertise lie in its people

00:51:35.720 --> 00:51:39.150
and in their memories
and in their experiences.

00:51:39.150 --> 00:51:44.150
So one needs to hire, train, mentor,

00:51:44.240 --> 00:51:48.610
get them out there in the field
and they will be brilliant.

00:51:48.610 --> 00:51:51.090
But for that, you need resourcing.

00:51:51.090 --> 00:51:54.850
So number one is resource it.

00:51:54.850 --> 00:51:58.630
Number two is we have some
extraordinary legacies.

00:51:58.630 --> 00:52:02.200
I mean, what happened
with HIV AIDS and PEPFAR

00:52:02.200 --> 00:52:07.200
is a story of health that
everyone is proud of.

00:52:07.830 --> 00:52:10.170
Polio, we are so close.

00:52:10.170 --> 00:52:12.940
I mean, we are really close.

00:52:12.940 --> 00:52:15.840
We could get this done
in the next few years.

00:52:15.840 --> 00:52:19.000
And if we could rid this world of polio,

00:52:19.000 --> 00:52:21.380
it would be a great achievement.

00:52:21.380 --> 00:52:26.380
So focus on a few things
that would change the world.

00:52:26.790 --> 00:52:29.550
And that is always important.

00:52:29.550 --> 00:52:31.760
Whether the institution is USAID

00:52:31.760 --> 00:52:33.650
or whether it is UNICEF.

00:52:33.650 --> 00:52:37.580
But USAID carries the flag of America

00:52:37.580 --> 00:52:42.580
and thus American institutions,
corporations and foundations

00:52:43.010 --> 00:52:47.310
have a chance to showcase
many of their innovations,

00:52:47.310 --> 00:52:52.310
their digital creations
that will change our world.

00:52:54.510 --> 00:52:57.510
COVID means that every country stops

00:52:57.510 --> 00:53:02.510
and re looks at its
budget and it reallocates.

00:53:02.530 --> 00:53:04.860
So let us make the case

00:53:04.860 --> 00:53:08.650
that we reallocated for a better world,

00:53:08.650 --> 00:53:11.390
a world that includes everyone

00:53:11.390 --> 00:53:13.260
and that there are no children

00:53:13.260 --> 00:53:15.480
or grown-ups that are left behind.

00:53:15.480 --> 00:53:19.690
And if we can do that as a
world, it will be a better place.

00:53:19.690 --> 00:53:23.720
And USAID will do its share and it's part,

00:53:23.720 --> 00:53:27.260
but it is all hands on deck side, Saad.

00:53:27.260 --> 00:53:32.260
And as whether you're a USAID
or UNICEF or anyone else,

00:53:32.510 --> 00:53:34.110
we need everybody out there.

00:53:34.110 --> 00:53:35.630
We also need to make the case

00:53:35.630 --> 00:53:40.630
that what happens in the world
matters to people in America.

00:53:40.750 --> 00:53:44.590
And sometimes one thinks
that one's own household

00:53:44.590 --> 00:53:46.044
or one's own community

00:53:46.044 --> 00:53:49.250
is the limits to what you can affect

00:53:49.250 --> 00:53:52.320
but one person can make a big difference.

00:53:52.320 --> 00:53:57.300
And if you keep in mind the
needs of the world around you

00:53:57.300 --> 00:54:00.370
you'll see that there is a
world out there that needs help

00:54:00.370 --> 00:54:03.300
and that you, as one person can do a lot

00:54:03.300 --> 00:54:05.810
or you as a big important agency

00:54:05.810 --> 00:54:09.610
like USAID can help change the world.

00:54:09.610 --> 00:54:12.240
- So I'll take the liberty
of sort of before I thank you

00:54:12.240 --> 00:54:14.003
to ask you one last question.

00:54:15.430 --> 00:54:16.510
There will be

00:54:16.510 --> 00:54:21.000
you know, a big catastrophic
or consequential events

00:54:21.000 --> 00:54:26.000
in general have changed
the international system.

00:54:26.560 --> 00:54:29.750
The second world war changed
the international system,

00:54:29.750 --> 00:54:33.060
the great recession, or at
least the great depression

00:54:34.333 --> 00:54:38.870
had an impact on the international system.

00:54:38.870 --> 00:54:40.820
This is an event.

00:54:40.820 --> 00:54:44.130
This pandemic is an event
that impacts everyone.

00:54:44.130 --> 00:54:48.740
And you sort of function
within the UN ecosystem.

00:54:48.740 --> 00:54:51.053
How does the international system change?

00:54:52.020 --> 00:54:54.250
How does it change for the better?

00:54:54.250 --> 00:54:56.870
Because you have had a
somewhat unique perspective

00:54:56.870 --> 00:54:58.560
that you have led USAID.

00:54:58.560 --> 00:55:00.590
Obviously you talked about USAID's role,

00:55:00.590 --> 00:55:03.900
but also, you know UNICEF.

00:55:03.900 --> 00:55:06.970
So both a country perspective
and a global perspective,

00:55:06.970 --> 00:55:11.100
how does the the
international system change

00:55:11.100 --> 00:55:12.513
for the better, hopefully?

00:55:14.550 --> 00:55:18.555
- Well, there are a couple
of areas that I think

00:55:18.555 --> 00:55:20.270
are big opportunities for us.

00:55:20.270 --> 00:55:25.270
One is we do not yet
really work as one team

00:55:25.910 --> 00:55:30.350
on the public sector side or
in the private sector side.

00:55:30.350 --> 00:55:33.920
And it's due to a lot
of different reasons,

00:55:33.920 --> 00:55:36.780
but COVID does remind you

00:55:36.780 --> 00:55:40.820
that the great research
institutions are private.

00:55:40.820 --> 00:55:45.820
The corporations that are
creating the vaccines are private

00:55:46.060 --> 00:55:48.160
but many of the distribution systems

00:55:48.160 --> 00:55:50.520
I mean, UNICEF, is part of this

00:55:50.520 --> 00:55:52.580
in procuring and distributing

00:55:52.580 --> 00:55:54.920
could be a mix of public and private.

00:55:54.920 --> 00:55:56.020
And then when you get out

00:55:56.020 --> 00:55:59.790
into the field into the most
rural and remote communities

00:55:59.790 --> 00:56:03.360
it tends to be almost completely public.

00:56:03.360 --> 00:56:05.630
But couldn't, we reimagine a world

00:56:05.630 --> 00:56:09.300
in which there is a public
and private agreement

00:56:09.300 --> 00:56:12.930
on how to collaborate
in a much better way.

00:56:12.930 --> 00:56:15.020
I think COVID can teach us this.

00:56:15.020 --> 00:56:18.020
I think this is the moment for that.

00:56:18.020 --> 00:56:21.050
And if I can bring up education again,

00:56:21.050 --> 00:56:24.010
this is our time to connect
every school in the world,

00:56:24.010 --> 00:56:25.000
to the internet.

00:56:25.000 --> 00:56:29.100
If we can do that, it will
give a level playing field

00:56:29.100 --> 00:56:31.510
for every young person in the world.

00:56:31.510 --> 00:56:33.950
So they can all start at the same place.

00:56:33.950 --> 00:56:37.940
If not, we will break
into haves and have nots

00:56:37.940 --> 00:56:41.390
in a really unequal way.

00:56:41.390 --> 00:56:45.030
And so this would be a great
equalizer in the world.

00:56:45.030 --> 00:56:49.390
And if we could look
for those big important

00:56:49.390 --> 00:56:53.270
once in a generation
opportunities, that will be big.

00:56:53.270 --> 00:56:54.850
And then lastly, Saad,

00:56:54.850 --> 00:56:56.470
you knew that I'd have to say something

00:56:56.470 --> 00:56:57.520
for the young people,

00:56:57.520 --> 00:56:59.900
but the young people want to help.

00:56:59.900 --> 00:57:02.380
They don't want to be the beneficiaries.

00:57:02.380 --> 00:57:04.797
They want to be the actors,

00:57:04.797 --> 00:57:07.320
they want to do it themselves.

00:57:07.320 --> 00:57:09.760
So as we're doing our planning

00:57:09.760 --> 00:57:11.650
couldn't we have some young people

00:57:11.650 --> 00:57:16.280
on every commission in
every forum advising us?

00:57:16.280 --> 00:57:18.840
We started something
called Generation Unlimited

00:57:18.840 --> 00:57:20.470
there're young people on the board,

00:57:20.470 --> 00:57:22.310
there're young people advising us.

00:57:22.310 --> 00:57:23.840
We have young volunteers

00:57:23.840 --> 00:57:27.570
we have 2 million right now,
we're heading to 20 million.

00:57:27.570 --> 00:57:31.610
I mean, young people have
to be included in this.

00:57:31.610 --> 00:57:32.960
We're all in this together.

00:57:32.960 --> 00:57:35.577
And I think we will come out of COVID

00:57:35.577 --> 00:57:38.980
and this time empowering young people

00:57:38.980 --> 00:57:41.010
in a new and different way.

00:57:41.010 --> 00:57:46.010
So public, private, and young
people and collaboration.

00:57:46.890 --> 00:57:49.260
- So that's a good segue
into what I was gonna say

00:57:49.260 --> 00:57:51.090
that you know, that's,

00:57:51.090 --> 00:57:52.980
there are certain good things about Zoom

00:57:52.980 --> 00:57:57.090
that you don't have to
sort of drive up I-95

00:57:57.090 --> 00:57:59.820
to come from New York to New Haven,

00:57:59.820 --> 00:58:01.950
but then there are drawbacks as well

00:58:01.950 --> 00:58:05.960
that we can't have real two-way
interactions, et cetera.

00:58:05.960 --> 00:58:08.520
So hopefully when things settle down

00:58:08.520 --> 00:58:13.330
perhaps next year or in,
you know, 14, 18 months

00:58:13.330 --> 00:58:14.780
you would find time to come back

00:58:14.780 --> 00:58:17.410
where you could interact with our faculty

00:58:17.410 --> 00:58:22.410
but more specifically our
students who are young and wise.

00:58:22.680 --> 00:58:26.220
And well, wise well beyond their years.

00:58:26.220 --> 00:58:30.820
And so, and I'm sure you'll
enjoy interacting with them.

00:58:30.820 --> 00:58:34.960
And so hopefully you know,
that's after, you know,

00:58:34.960 --> 00:58:38.180
as I said you know, I have
when this is over list

00:58:38.180 --> 00:58:41.786
and hopefully we are on
your when this is over list.

00:58:41.786 --> 00:58:45.430
Come back and have another interaction

00:58:45.430 --> 00:58:47.650
with the broader Yale community.

00:58:47.650 --> 00:58:48.630
So thank you for that.

00:58:48.630 --> 00:58:50.910
I wanna thank Dr. Linda Arnold.

00:58:50.910 --> 00:58:54.990
I wanna thank others
who helped us coordinate

00:58:54.990 --> 00:58:57.170
and put together this event.

00:58:57.170 --> 00:59:01.290
And I especially wanna
thank Director Fore,

00:59:01.290 --> 00:59:04.680
for, so not just her insightful comments

00:59:04.680 --> 00:59:07.970
but her passionate pleas

00:59:07.970 --> 00:59:11.610
for engaging young people
and looking forward

00:59:11.610 --> 00:59:14.760
and connecting the world
and focusing on building.

00:59:14.760 --> 00:59:18.100
If I could, you know,
ascribe a subtext to it,

00:59:18.100 --> 00:59:22.677
to building a more resilient world

00:59:22.677 --> 00:59:25.660
that every time there is a catastrophe

00:59:25.660 --> 00:59:28.720
disproportionately impacts the weakest

00:59:28.720 --> 00:59:30.570
and the youngest amongst us.

00:59:30.570 --> 00:59:34.180
So thanks for taking time to do this

00:59:34.180 --> 00:59:36.110
and thank you everyone

00:59:36.110 --> 00:59:38.130
for signing in to,

00:59:38.130 --> 00:59:41.093
for logging in and being a
part of this event, thanks.

